    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The season didn't just end in Toronto - it collapsed under the weight of expectations, leaving behind another spring of what-ifs and a front office that now has to answer for a club that finished 32-36-14, 8th in the Atlantic Division, with 78 points and a miserable L7. <strong>The Leafs are out, stamped with an e and a playoff seed of 15, which is nothing short of a warning label on a team that never found its footing.</strong> There were flashes, sure, but no spine, no sustained run, no evidence this roster could stop bleeding goals or string together enough nights of professional hockey to matter. That is the blunt truth. Now comes the hard part - not the summer talking points, but the decisions. Who stays? Who goes? Does the coaching staff survive the wreckage? Toronto is entering the offseason with its reputation bruised and its mandate clear: fix the structure, trim the dead weight, and find players who can handle pressure instead of disappearing under it. <strong>What happens next - on the bench, in free agency, and at the draft - will decide whether this was a bad year or the start of something worse.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 21, 2026"
      },
