    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "22-27",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "A club that spent the first seven weeks looking like it had forgotten how to finish has found a pulse at last. <strong>Wednesday's 2-1 win over the Yankees</strong> gave the Blue Jays a much-needed breath of air, and it came the right way - with pitching, nerve and a little bite. Toronto is 22-27, still third in the AL East and 11.5 games back, which means the hill is steep and the calendar is not exactly merciful. But a win like that matters because it followed a pair of one-run losses in the Bronx and showed the Jays can still survive a knife fight. Trey Yesavage was the headline, striking out eight in the kind of outing that quiets a crowd and steadies a season. The offense, as usual, was stingy - just enough, not nearly enough, but enough on this night. That is the story here: a team with a minus-2.5 run profile and a playoff seed sitting at 9 needs more than a spark, it needs a string. <strong>Tonight's rematch with the Yankees at 6:05 p.m. ET is the next test - and the next chance to prove Wednesday was a start, not a fluke.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated May 21, 2026"
      },
